<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Land of Hope and Glory</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">music <a href="/years/1901">1901</a>; words <a href="/years/1902">1902</a>; first sung June <a href="/years/1902">1902</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The tune is the broad &quot;trio&quot; melody of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-elgar">Edward Elgar</a>'s Pomp and Circumstance March No. 1, composed in 1901. King Edward VII told Elgar that the melody would make a great song, and when Elgar was asked to write a work for the King's coronation he took up the idea in his Coronation Ode, to words by the poet and essayist A. C. Benson, using the march's melody in its first and last sections.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>&quot;Land of Hope and Glory&quot; is an English patriotic song with music by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-elgar">Edward Elgar</a>, written in 1901, and lyrics by A. C. Benson added in 1902. Its music is the trio theme of Elgar's Pomp and Circumstance March No. 1. The words were fitted to the melody on the suggestion of King Edward VII, who told Elgar he thought the melody would make a great song.</p><p>When Elgar was asked to write a work for the king's coronation, he worked the suggestion into his Coronation Ode, with words by the poet and essayist Benson, and the first and last sections of the Ode use the march's melody. The coronation was postponed because of the king's illness, so Elgar created a separate song, which Madame Clara Butt first performed in June 1902.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Only the first of the seven stanzas of the Ode's final section was re-used for the song, and this is the part popularly sung today. The refrain begins &quot;Land of Hope and Glory, Mother of the Free&quot; and prays that God, who made the land mighty, will make it mightier yet. The first five notes of the refrain are similar to the first two bars of &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot; in an early version published in 1745. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> used the song as its victory anthem at the Commonwealth Games until 2010, when the public chose &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>&quot; in a poll.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The Proms began in 1895, and Elgar's march was introduced there as an orchestral piece in 1901, a year before the words were written, conducted by Henry Wood, who later recalled that he had little thought the broad melody of the trio would develop into &quot;our second national anthem&quot;. It was played as &quot;Land of Hope and Glory&quot; at the last concert of 1905 and at the first and last concerts of 1909. From 1927 the BBC supported the Proms with radio broadcasts, and from 1947 the conductor Malcolm Sargent made the song part of the standard programme of the Last Night, broadcast on television annually from 1953.</p><p>In 2020 the BBC announced that &quot;Land of Hope and Glory&quot; and &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-britannia">Rule, Britannia!</a>&quot; would not feature in the Last Night. Some members of the public, the media and politicians objected, among them Prime Minister Boris Johnson, who accused the BBC of &quot;cringing embarrassment about our history&quot;. Supporters of Black Lives Matter had accused the songs of glorifying colonialism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, but the BBC later said the original decision was due to COVID-19 social-distancing measures and reversed it; both songs were performed with a reduced choir and orchestra.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Land of Hope and Glory” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was played as &quot;<b>Land of Hope and Glory</b>&quot; at the last concert of 1905, and from 1916 to 1918 it followed Wood's Fantasia on British Sea Songs at the closing concerts.</blockquote><a href="/songs/land-of-hope-and-glory">Land of Hope and Glory</a></li><li><blockquote>In 2020 the BBC announced that &quot;Rule, Britannia!&quot; and &quot;<b>Land of Hope and Glory</b>&quot; would not be sung at the Last Night of the Proms.</blockquote><a href="/songs/rule-britannia">Rule, Britannia!</a></li></ul></section>
